      "problem": "Given that the continuous random variable $X$ is uniformly distributed on the interval $[1,5],$ compute $P(X>2).$",
      "worked_solution": "The probability density function for a uniformly distributed continuous random variable $X$ on an interval $[a,b]$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{b-a},\\, & a\\le x\\le b, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOur random variable $X$ is uniformly distributed on the interval $[1,5],$ so it has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4},\\, & 1\\le x\\le 5, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>2) & =P(2<X\\le 5) \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{5}\\frac{1}{4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{5-2}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
